From: Vorapol Rinsatitnon <vorapol.r@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 04 Sep 2026 17:22:25 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] internal/poll: keep file handles off a completion port they cannot leave

os.(*File).Fd guarantees that the handle it returns is not attached to the
runtime's completion port, so that a caller can attach it to one of their own
(CL 664455, Go 1.25). Before Windows 8.1 nothing can detach a handle from a
port, so the guarantee cannot be met.

	Init puts a file on the port  ->  File.Fd cannot take it off
	                              ->  caller's CreateIoCompletionPort refused

A handle that cannot be detached from a port must never be attached to one. On
those kernels Init keeps every handle except sockets off the poller, and such a
handle keeps its own deadlines. Sockets stay on the poller, because that is
what lets one process serve more connections than it has threads.

The cost is one thread per waiting operation, which is how Windows 7 file and
pipe I/O worked through Go 1.24 anyway.

diff --git a/src/internal/poll/fd_poll_runtime.go b/src/internal/poll/fd_poll_runtime.go
index 2aef1124..d5b7d54f 100644
--- a/src/internal/poll/fd_poll_runtime.go
+++ b/src/internal/poll/fd_poll_runtime.go
@@ -157,7 +157,8 @@ func setDeadlineImpl(fd *FD, t time.Time, mode int) error {
 	defer fd.decref()
 
 	if fd.pd.runtimeCtx == 0 {
-		return ErrNoDeadline
+		// Windows keeps some deadlines outside the poller.
+		return fd.setDeadlineNoPoller(d, mode)
 	}
 	runtime_pollSetDeadline(fd.pd.runtimeCtx, d, mode)
 	return nil
